Article 6: To apply for recognition of a civil judgment of a court in the Taiwan region, the following materials shall be submitted:
(1) a written application, together with copies to be submitted in accordance with the number of respondents;
(2) the original judgment or a certified copy;
(3) the original certificate of finality of the judgment or a certified copy, except that, pursuant to relevant regulations of the Taiwan region, mediation records and the like need not be accompanied by a separate certificate;
(4) identity certification materials (where the applicant is a natural person, copies of identity documents such as the resident identity card, Residence Permit for Taiwan Residents, Mainland Travel Permit for Taiwan Residents, etc. shall be submitted; where the applicant is a legal person or unincorporated organization, copies of the registration certificate and the identity documents of its legal representative or principal responsible person shall be submitted; where the applicant is a heir or assign of a party, materials proving the identity as such heir or assign shall be submitted).
Where identity certification materials are formed outside Mainland China, the applicant shall complete certification procedures pursuant to the Civil Procedure Law and relevant judicial interpretations.

Article 7: The written application shall state the following matters:
(1) basic information of the applicant and the respondent: where the applicant or the respondent is a natural person, including name, domicile, identity document information, and means of communication; where the applicant or the respondent is a legal person or unincorporated organization, including the name, domicile, and the name, position, domicile, identity document information, and means of communication of its legal representative or principal responsible person;
(2) the name of the court in the Taiwan region that rendered the judgment, the case number of the judicial document, the date of commencement of the litigation proceedings, and the date of the judgment;
(3) the claims and the reasons;
(4) the enforcement status of the judgment for which recognition is sought;
(5) other circumstances that need to be stated.

Article 16: A civil judgment of a court in the Taiwan region shall be ruled not to be recognized if it falls under any of the following circumstances:
(1) the civil judgment for which recognition is sought was rendered when the respondent was in default and had not been lawfully summoned, or when the respondent lacked capacity to conduct litigation and had not been properly represented;
(2) the case is subject to exclusive jurisdiction of the People's Court;
(3) the parties to the case have concluded a valid arbitration agreement and there are no circumstances of waiver of arbitration jurisdiction;
(4) the judgment was obtained by fraud;
(5) the People's Court has already rendered a judgment or ruling on the same dispute, or has already recognized a judgment or ruling rendered by a court of another country or region on the same dispute;
(6) an arbitral tribunal has already rendered an arbitral award in Mainland China on the same dispute, or the People's Court has already recognized an arbitral award rendered by an arbitral tribunal in another country or region on the same dispute.
Where recognition of the civil judgment would violate the basic principles of national laws, such as the One-China principle, or would harm national sovereignty, security, or social public interests, the People's Court shall rule not to recognize the judgment.
